WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
Python doesn't really know how to represent this generator in any other way. It doesn't know what's in it until you start looping through it.

00:07.000 --> 00:14.000
The other cool thing about generators is they don't necessarily have to have an end, right?

00:14.000 --> 00:20.000
The structure is not in memory, so you can kind of be a little loosey goosey about it.

00:20.000 --> 00:27.000
A good way to think about generators is, you know, the list has all the items in it,

00:27.000 --> 00:32.000
but a generator and only knows where it is right now, and it knows the very next step.

00:32.000 --> 00:37.000
It has no idea what happened in the past. It's got, you know, some weird amnesia,

00:37.000 --> 00:42.000
and it doesn't really know where it's going in the future besides for the very next step.

00:42.000 --> 00:46.000
So I'll make a little bit more sense.

00:46.000 --> 00:56.000
We talked a little bit about slicing yesterday, but slicing is a way to create sublifts.

00:57.000 --> 01:04.000
From larger lists. So let's say I had my string.

01:04.000 --> 01:15.000
So our string is kind of considered as sequence in Python.

01:15.000 --> 01:19.000
What well my string zero return?

01:19.000 --> 01:23.000
Each.

01:23.000 --> 01:30.000
We also looked at negative slicing. What well my string subscripts negative one return?

01:30.000 --> 01:34.000
exclamation point.

01:34.000 --> 01:39.000
But we also learned that we can provide two numbers into this range.

01:39.000 --> 01:42.000
So let's just say I wanted to return the string, hello.

01:42.000 --> 01:49.000
I can say zero one two three four.

01:49.000 --> 01:57.000
And I would put in position five because just like a range.

01:57.000 --> 01:59.000
These are the first four characters.

01:59.000 --> 02:05.000
Just like range, the second argument to the subscript, it's exclusive.

02:05.000 --> 02:09.000
So it does not include the character that you put in there.

02:09.000 --> 02:17.000
We also know that when you're working with either the first value of the list or the last value of the list,

02:17.000 --> 02:24.000
that the zero is optional gives us the same thing.

02:24.000 --> 02:30.000
So we can put any numbers in here that we want.

02:30.000 --> 02:37.000
It'll give us a new copy of those values from the string.

02:37.000 --> 02:45.000
So doing it with a list.

02:45.000 --> 02:49.000
Renders the same results.

02:49.000 --> 02:52.000
Say I have a list of four items in it.

02:52.000 --> 02:55.000
What will name zero return?

02:55.000 --> 02:59.000
And names negative one.

03:00.000 --> 03:05.000
What's the last item?

03:05.000 --> 03:10.000
What if I wanted the first two items in the list?

03:10.000 --> 03:14.000
What would I put for my second range variable?

03:14.000 --> 03:18.000
First two items would be three.

03:18.000 --> 03:20.000
Oh sorry, two.

03:20.000 --> 03:23.000
I'm sorry, whoever corrected me, you were right.

03:23.000 --> 03:28.000
Because list indexes start at zero.

03:28.000 --> 03:35.000
And is this zero optional or not?

03:35.000 --> 03:43.000
If I wanted to go to the end of the string, I would start at zero one two.

03:43.000 --> 03:49.000
And I would go to four.

03:49.000 --> 03:51.000
So that's the end of my list.

03:51.000 --> 03:53.000
That's the length of it.

03:53.000 --> 03:57.000
Just like what zero is this four optional.

03:57.000 --> 04:03.000
It is because we're going all the way to the end.

04:03.000 --> 04:08.000
One of the cool useful uses for the substring.

04:08.000 --> 04:12.000
So if we have a list, let's say my list names,

04:12.000 --> 04:16.000
and I reassign it to new names.

04:17.000 --> 04:23.000
If I append a new value here,

04:23.000 --> 04:28.000
is it going to change my names list?

04:28.000 --> 04:32.000
Well, yes.

04:32.000 --> 04:33.000
It will, right?

04:33.000 --> 04:37.000
Because all I did is I changed the label here.

04:37.000 --> 04:39.000
I didn't make a new list, right?

04:39.000 --> 04:42.000
I just told Python, these things share a name now.

04:42.000 --> 04:45.000
The point at the same thing.

04:46.000 --> 04:49.000
Can you print out names, too?

04:49.000 --> 04:51.000
It's the same.

04:51.000 --> 04:55.000
So if I quickly wanted to make a copy of my list,

04:55.000 --> 05:01.000
so that I can do some operations on it and not modify the original,

05:01.000 --> 05:10.000
let's say I had my list of names here.

05:11.000 --> 05:14.000
If we started the beginning of a sequence,

05:14.000 --> 05:18.000
and we want to take a slice, is the zero optional?

05:18.000 --> 05:20.000
Yes.

05:20.000 --> 05:23.000
If we go to the end of the sequence,

05:23.000 --> 05:27.000
is the last number, that last value, is that optional?

05:27.000 --> 05:28.000
It is.

05:28.000 --> 05:34.000
So if I put just the colon in here, I'll get a copy of my list.

05:35.000 --> 05:41.000
And now I can say, new names is equal to a copy of my names list.

05:41.000 --> 05:45.000
Sorry, we got new names, but bear with me.

05:45.000 --> 05:53.000
If I append something to this, now, will my names list change?

05:53.000 --> 05:55.000
No.

05:55.000 --> 06:01.000
But my new names, I modified that, right?

06:04.000 --> 06:05.000
Okay.

06:05.000 --> 06:12.000
Let's talk about negative indexing a little bit.

06:12.000 --> 06:19.000
So I had my string here, hello, world.

06:19.000 --> 06:25.000
If I do a negative index one, what do I get?

06:25.000 --> 06:31.000
What about negative index two?

06:31.000 --> 06:36.000
Same where I'm getting here.

06:36.000 --> 06:37.000
Yeah.

06:37.000 --> 06:43.000
So negative one goes to the first last item, negative two,

06:43.000 --> 06:46.000
kind of et cetera, et cetera.

06:46.000 --> 06:50.000
You can get kind of interesting here.

06:50.000 --> 06:54.000
You can go from the end, a length of the string,

06:54.000 --> 07:00.000
to the fourth from the end, and end up with some,

07:01.000 --> 07:03.000
some subset of the string.

07:03.000 --> 07:06.000
You can do the same thing for lists.

07:06.000 --> 07:11.000
You kind of rarely want to get too tricky with the substrings,

07:11.000 --> 07:13.000
because explicit is better than implicit.

07:13.000 --> 07:16.000
You want to be clear, but it's available to you,

07:16.000 --> 07:19.000
and you might see this in Python code.

07:19.000 --> 07:21.000
Okay.

07:21.000 --> 07:29.000
One last argument, let's say I had a list of the numbers from zero to nine.

07:29.000 --> 07:34.000
I can quickly build that list with range, right?

07:34.000 --> 07:39.000
And pass it in to list, and just looking at our list,

07:39.000 --> 07:44.000
the numbers from zero to nine.

07:44.000 --> 07:48.000
We know that this will give us the whole list.

07:48.000 --> 07:51.000
There's one additional third argument to step.

07:51.000 --> 07:54.000
And you can kind of think about it like range.

07:54.000 --> 07:57.000
So the first argument to range, the optional first argument

07:57.000 --> 07:58.000
is the start.

07:58.000 --> 08:01.000
The second argument to range is the end.

08:01.000 --> 08:05.000
What's the third optional argument to range?

08:05.000 --> 08:07.000
It's a step.

08:07.000 --> 08:09.000
Yeah, interval or a step.

08:09.000 --> 08:12.000
So I can do the same thing here,

08:12.000 --> 08:17.000
and say, just give me every other one.

08:17.000 --> 08:20.000
One little other bit of trickery here.

08:20.000 --> 08:22.000
What if the step was negative one?

08:22.000 --> 08:26.000
What do you think that would do?

08:27.000 --> 08:29.000
That's right.

08:29.000 --> 08:30.000
Yeah.

08:30.000 --> 08:33.000
So going through the whole list,

08:33.000 --> 08:36.000
stepping from the end to the beginning.

08:36.000 --> 08:40.000
So just a little bit of trickery there.

08:40.000 --> 08:50.000
Let's say I wanted to go start from the first value.

08:50.000 --> 08:54.000
And let's look at my list again here.

08:54.000 --> 08:57.000
I wanted to start from the first value.

08:57.000 --> 09:00.000
Go to the sixth one.

09:00.000 --> 09:03.000
And then skip every other.

09:03.000 --> 09:12.000
I could use all three of those arguments to range.

09:12.000 --> 09:17.000
And remember that you can slice to get a subset of items from any data type that

09:17.000 --> 09:19.000
maintains an order.

09:19.000 --> 09:22.000
So that would be your list and your tuple.

09:22.000 --> 09:30.000
Can you do this sort of sub sequencing with a dictionary or set?

09:30.000 --> 09:32.000
No, right?

09:32.000 --> 09:34.000
Why?

09:34.000 --> 09:37.000
They're unordered, right?

09:37.000 --> 09:40.000
So if you can't for the data structure,

09:40.000 --> 09:43.000
if you can't subscript it by an index.

09:43.000 --> 09:46.000
And remember, you can do this with a dictionary.

09:46.000 --> 09:48.000
Only if the dictionary has a key of zero.

09:48.000 --> 09:49.000
So that doesn't count.

09:49.000 --> 09:53.000
If you can do this to the data type, then you can take a slice of that.

